RetroBSD libmagic database
==========================

`magic` is a size-conscious selection from the upstream file 5.47 Magdir
database.  It covers executable and object formats, archives, compression,
filesystems, firmware, disk partitioning, documents, images, audio/video,
crypto material, source and script languages, and the common formats used
when developing RetroBSD.

Selected upstream fragments:

    aout archive audio cafebabe c-lang commands compress crypto database der
    diff elf filesystems firmware fonts gcc git gpt images javascript jpeg
    linux lua mach magic mail.news make mips msdos msooxml ole2compounddocs
    pdf perl pgp pgp-binary-keys python riff rtf ruby rust securitycerts sgml
    ssh ssl subtitle tcl tex timezone unicode uuencode varied.out varied.script
    web webassembly windows wordprocessors zip

`magic.mgc.little` and `magic.mgc.big` were compiled from that text using
file 5.47 and `magic-endian.c`.  Supplying a native-endian database matters:
although libmagic can byte-swap a foreign database, doing so would dirty every
page of a private mapping.  Native databases let RetroBSD keep the mapping
read-only, shared, and demand-paged instead of copying rules into the process
heap.  Clean rule pages can therefore be discarded and faulted back from the
database without consuming swap.

The input side is bounded separately.  For regular files smaller than
`FILE_BYTES_MAX`, libmagic allocates only the actual file size rather than the
full seven-megabyte inspection ceiling.

To rebuild after editing `magic`, use a host file 5.47:

    make FILE_HOST=/path/to/file-5.47 regen-magic
